# BuddyNet VPS firewall (iptables), the iptables-restore equivalent of
# nftables.conf: default-drop input, with only SSH and the two BuddyNet UDP
# ports open. The UDP ports are rate-limited to blunt floods.
#
# Edit the two BuddyNet dports below to match your --listen settings, then:
#   sudo iptables-restore < deployments/iptables.rules
#   # persist with iptables-persistent / netfilter-persistent
#
# For IPv6 use the same file with ip6tables-restore (adjust the ICMP line to
# icmpv6 as noted). WARNING: input policy is DROP; SSH (22) is allowed below so
# you are not locked out — change it if your SSH runs elsewhere.
*filter
:INPUT DROP [0:0]
:FORWARD DROP [0:0]
:OUTPUT ACCEPT [0:0]

-A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -m conntrack --ctstate INVALID -j DROP

# BuddyNet's UDP ports come BEFORE the generic ESTABLISHED,RELATED accept.
# Conntrack marks a UDP flow established as soon as it has seen traffic both ways,
# so once the server answers the first packet, every later packet of that 5-tuple
# matched the accept and the limit was never reached. The excess must also be
# dropped explicitly, or it falls through to the established rule.
-A INPUT -p udp --dport 51820 -m limit --limit 100/second --limit-burst 50 -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -p udp --dport 51820 -j DROP

# The relay port has NO packet-rate limit on purpose: it carries tunnel DATA, and
# a rate safe for a control plane would throttle the tunnel. Its ceilings live in
# the relay itself; bandwidth is a shaping/provider problem, not a packets-per-
# second rule. Restrict WHO may reach it (--allow-cidr, or remove this line and
# firewall the port to your buddies).
-A INPUT -p udp --dport 51821 -j ACCEPT

# Everything else that belongs to an existing conversation.
-A INPUT -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT

# ICMP (use -p icmpv6 with ip6tables-restore).
-A INPUT -p icmp -j ACCEPT

# Management.
-A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT

COMMIT
